       The _t_e_r_m_c_a_p functions provide  no  means  of  freeing  memory,  because
       legacy  _t_e_r_m_c_a_p  implementations used only the buffer areas provided by
       the caller via ttggeetteenntt and ttggeettssttrr.  Those buffers are unused in  _t_e_r_m_‐
       _i_n_f_o.

       By  contrast,  _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o allocates memory.  It uses sseettuupptteerrmm(3X) to ob‐
       tain the data used by ttggeetteenntt and the functions that retrieve  capabil‐
       ity values.  One could use
              del_curterm(cur_term);
       to  free  this  memory,  but  there  is an additional complication with
       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s.  It uses a fixed-size pool of storage locations, one per value
       of the terminal name parameter given to ttggeetteenntt.  The _s_c_r_e_e_n(1) program
       relies upon this arrangement to improve its performance.

